  ('ai:ai-batch-06:e505ebc8e6bd','C1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','She ___ the significance of the findings, saying they were preliminary.',array['played out','played up','played off','played down']::text[],'D','''Play down'' means make seem less important.',array['exam','vocabulary']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:3d0e8b47810d','C1'::public.cefr_level,'vocabulary','The government''s response was criticised as ___ and too late.',array['half-hearted','light-hearted','big-hearted','kind-hearted']::text[],'A','Half-hearted = without enthusiasm or effort.',array['exam','vocabulary']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:b3eddbf567f9','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is a ''nudge'', according to the text?',array['a type of pension','a change in how choices are presented','a ban on certain products','a new tax']::text[],'B','''Small changes in how choices are presented.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Governments have increasingly turned to ''nudges'' — small changes in how choices are presented — to influence behaviour without banning anything or raising prices. Making pension saving the default option, for instance, so that employees must actively opt out rather than opt in, has dramatically increased participation in the UK. Advocates describe this as ''libertarian paternalism'': people remain free to choose, yet are steered towards decisions that serve their long-term interests. Critics, however, question who decides what those interests are, and warn that techniques which work precisely because people do not notice them sit uneasily with democratic transparency. Others argue that nudges are too modest to tackle problems such as obesity or climate change, and may serve as a convenient excuse for avoiding more effective but less popular regulation.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:c4b426757ba7','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How did automatic pension enrolment change behaviour?',array['More employees saved for pensions.','Fewer people opted out of work.','Pensions became compulsory.','Employers stopped paying into pensions.']::text[],'A','Making saving the default ''dramatically increased participation''.',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Governments have increasingly turned to ''nudges'' — small changes in how choices are presented — to influence behaviour without banning anything or raising prices. Making pension saving the default option, for instance, so that employees must actively opt out rather than opt in, has dramatically increased participation in the UK. Advocates describe this as ''libertarian paternalism'': people remain free to choose, yet are steered towards decisions that serve their long-term interests. Critics, however, question who decides what those interests are, and warn that techniques which work precisely because people do not notice them sit uneasily with democratic transparency. Others argue that nudges are too modest to tackle problems such as obesity or climate change, and may serve as a convenient excuse for avoiding more effective but less popular regulation.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:fc97bc8d0e5b','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What concern about democracy do critics raise?',array['Nudges remove all choice.','Nudges work because people don''t notice them.','Nudges are decided by voters.','Nudges are too expensive.']::text[],'B','''Techniques which work precisely because people do not notice them sit uneasily with democratic transparency.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Governments have increasingly turned to ''nudges'' — small changes in how choices are presented — to influence behaviour without banning anything or raising prices. Making pension saving the default option, for instance, so that employees must actively opt out rather than opt in, has dramatically increased participation in the UK. Advocates describe this as ''libertarian paternalism'': people remain free to choose, yet are steered towards decisions that serve their long-term interests. Critics, however, question who decides what those interests are, and warn that techniques which work precisely because people do not notice them sit uneasily with democratic transparency. Others argue that nudges are too modest to tackle problems such as obesity or climate change, and may serve as a convenient excuse for avoiding more effective but less popular regulation.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:734995f852f2','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What do some argue nudges may be used as?',array['a way to raise taxes','a tool only for pensions','a replacement for elections','an excuse to avoid stronger regulation']::text[],'D','''May serve as a convenient excuse for avoiding more effective but less popular regulation.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Governments have increasingly turned to ''nudges'' — small changes in how choices are presented — to influence behaviour without banning anything or raising prices. Making pension saving the default option, for instance, so that employees must actively opt out rather than opt in, has dramatically increased participation in the UK. Advocates describe this as ''libertarian paternalism'': people remain free to choose, yet are steered towards decisions that serve their long-term interests. Critics, however, question who decides what those interests are, and warn that techniques which work precisely because people do not notice them sit uneasily with democratic transparency. Others argue that nudges are too modest to tackle problems such as obesity or climate change, and may serve as a convenient excuse for avoiding more effective but less popular regulation.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:0c9f81849e9d','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What does ''libertarian paternalism'' mean in the text?',array['letting the market decide everything','guiding choices while leaving people free to choose','forcing people to act in their interest','banning harmful products']::text[],'B','''People remain free to choose, yet are steered towards decisions that serve their long-term interests.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Governments have increasingly turned to ''nudges'' — small changes in how choices are presented — to influence behaviour without banning anything or raising prices. Making pension saving the default option, for instance, so that employees must actively opt out rather than opt in, has dramatically increased participation in the UK. Advocates describe this as ''libertarian paternalism'': people remain free to choose, yet are steered towards decisions that serve their long-term interests. Critics, however, question who decides what those interests are, and warn that techniques which work precisely because people do not notice them sit uneasily with democratic transparency. Others argue that nudges are too modest to tackle problems such as obesity or climate change, and may serve as a convenient excuse for avoiding more effective but less popular regulation.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:37fe3cdc5629','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why do some translators keep unfamiliar words and syntax?',array['because they lack time','to make the book easier to read','to remind readers they are meeting another culture','because publishers insist on it']::text[],'C','''Readers... should be reminded that they are encountering another culture.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Literary translators are frequently described as invisible, and the best translations are often praised for reading as though they had been written in the target language. Yet this ideal of seamlessness is itself contested. Some translators deliberately retain unfamiliar syntax or untranslated words, arguing that smoothing away every trace of the original amounts to a kind of cultural erasure, presenting foreign experience in comfortably domestic terms. Readers, they suggest, should be reminded that they are encountering another culture. Publishers, mindful of sales, tend to favour fluency, and translators'' names still rarely appear on book covers, though campaigns in recent years have begun to change this.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:4c1043630e04','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What do these translators consider a form of ''cultural erasure''?',array['leaving words untranslated','translating poetry','smoothing away all traces of the original','putting translators'' names on covers']::text[],'C','''Smoothing away every trace of the original amounts to a kind of cultural erasure.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Literary translators are frequently described as invisible, and the best translations are often praised for reading as though they had been written in the target language. Yet this ideal of seamlessness is itself contested. Some translators deliberately retain unfamiliar syntax or untranslated words, arguing that smoothing away every trace of the original amounts to a kind of cultural erasure, presenting foreign experience in comfortably domestic terms. Readers, they suggest, should be reminded that they are encountering another culture. Publishers, mindful of sales, tend to favour fluency, and translators'' names still rarely appear on book covers, though campaigns in recent years have begun to change this.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:e1523ee9631a','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why do publishers tend to favour fluent translations?',array['They are concerned about sales.','They are cheaper to produce.','They are required by law.','Translators prefer them.']::text[],'A','''Publishers, mindful of sales, tend to favour fluency.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Literary translators are frequently described as invisible, and the best translations are often praised for reading as though they had been written in the target language. Yet this ideal of seamlessness is itself contested. Some translators deliberately retain unfamiliar syntax or untranslated words, arguing that smoothing away every trace of the original amounts to a kind of cultural erasure, presenting foreign experience in comfortably domestic terms. Readers, they suggest, should be reminded that they are encountering another culture. Publishers, mindful of sales, tend to favour fluency, and translators'' names still rarely appear on book covers, though campaigns in recent years have begun to change this.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:fd85813a0d85','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What has recently begun to change?',array['Translations sell better than originals.','Readers prefer unfamiliar syntax.','Publishers have stopped translating.','Translators'' names are appearing on covers more often.']::text[],'D','''Translators'' names still rarely appear on book covers, though campaigns... have begun to change this.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Literary translators are frequently described as invisible, and the best translations are often praised for reading as though they had been written in the target language. Yet this ideal of seamlessness is itself contested. Some translators deliberately retain unfamiliar syntax or untranslated words, arguing that smoothing away every trace of the original amounts to a kind of cultural erasure, presenting foreign experience in comfortably domestic terms. Readers, they suggest, should be reminded that they are encountering another culture. Publishers, mindful of sales, tend to favour fluency, and translators'' names still rarely appear on book covers, though campaigns in recent years have begun to change this.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:be6f0ac5d599','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why do city residents typically use less energy per person?',array['city people are richer','cities are colder','cities have cleaner power stations','smaller homes, shorter journeys and more public transport']::text[],'D','They ''live in smaller homes that share walls, travel shorter distances and are more likely to walk or use public transport.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','The notion that cities are inherently bad for the environment has been challenged by urban economists. Per person, residents of dense cities typically consume less energy than suburban or rural dwellers: they live in smaller homes that share walls, travel shorter distances and are more likely to walk or use public transport. The difficulty is that density is frequently unpopular with existing residents, who object to taller buildings and fear congestion and the loss of green space. Planning systems that give considerable weight to local objections may therefore, paradoxically, push development outwards, generating precisely the car-dependent sprawl that environmental policy seeks to avoid.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:985a7bee2770','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What paradox does the writer describe?',array['Green spaces increase traffic.','Tall buildings save no energy.','Local objections can cause more car-dependent sprawl.','Dense cities use more energy than villages.']::text[],'C','Giving weight to local objections ''may... push development outwards, generating... sprawl''.',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','The notion that cities are inherently bad for the environment has been challenged by urban economists. Per person, residents of dense cities typically consume less energy than suburban or rural dwellers: they live in smaller homes that share walls, travel shorter distances and are more likely to walk or use public transport. The difficulty is that density is frequently unpopular with existing residents, who object to taller buildings and fear congestion and the loss of green space. Planning systems that give considerable weight to local objections may therefore, paradoxically, push development outwards, generating precisely the car-dependent sprawl that environmental policy seeks to avoid.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:ef7847ffec02','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why is density often unpopular with existing residents?',array['It raises energy bills.','It reduces public transport.','It forces them to move.','They fear taller buildings, congestion and lost green space.']::text[],'D','''Existing residents... object to taller buildings and fear congestion and the loss of green space.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','The notion that cities are inherently bad for the environment has been challenged by urban economists. Per person, residents of dense cities typically consume less energy than suburban or rural dwellers: they live in smaller homes that share walls, travel shorter distances and are more likely to walk or use public transport. The difficulty is that density is frequently unpopular with existing residents, who object to taller buildings and fear congestion and the loss of green space. Planning systems that give considerable weight to local objections may therefore, paradoxically, push development outwards, generating precisely the car-dependent sprawl that environmental policy seeks to avoid.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:4192f8df7407','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What does the writer imply about the idea that cities are bad for the environment?',array['It is too simple.','It applies only to old cities.','It was invented by economists.','It is completely correct.']::text[],'A','The notion ''has been challenged'' with evidence about energy use per person.',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','The notion that cities are inherently bad for the environment has been challenged by urban economists. Per person, residents of dense cities typically consume less energy than suburban or rural dwellers: they live in smaller homes that share walls, travel shorter distances and are more likely to walk or use public transport. The difficulty is that density is frequently unpopular with existing residents, who object to taller buildings and fear congestion and the loss of green space. Planning systems that give considerable weight to local objections may therefore, paradoxically, push development outwards, generating precisely the car-dependent sprawl that environmental policy seeks to avoid.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:220f71ef7fb0','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the ''replication crisis''?',array['Many published results couldn''t be reproduced.','Journals published too few studies.','Scientists copied each other''s work.','Experiments became too expensive.']::text[],'A','''A surprisingly large proportion of published findings... could not be reproduced.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','The ''replication crisis'' refers to the discovery that a surprisingly large proportion of published findings, particularly in psychology and medicine, could not be reproduced when other researchers repeated the experiments. Several factors have been blamed. Journals have historically preferred striking, positive results, giving researchers an incentive to analyse their data in many ways until something appears significant. Small sample sizes make chance findings more likely. In response, many journals now encourage ''pre-registration'', in which scientists publicly state their hypotheses and methods before collecting data, making it harder to present an exploratory fishing expedition as a confirmed prediction.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:ac183c325359','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How did journals'' preferences contribute to the problem?',array['They published only replications.','They favoured striking, positive results.','They rejected all psychology papers.','They demanded very large samples.']::text[],'B','''Journals have historically preferred striking, positive results.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','The ''replication crisis'' refers to the discovery that a surprisingly large proportion of published findings, particularly in psychology and medicine, could not be reproduced when other researchers repeated the experiments. Several factors have been blamed. Journals have historically preferred striking, positive results, giving researchers an incentive to analyse their data in many ways until something appears significant. Small sample sizes make chance findings more likely. In response, many journals now encourage ''pre-registration'', in which scientists publicly state their hypotheses and methods before collecting data, making it harder to present an exploratory fishing expedition as a confirmed prediction.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:2fc3283afdcd','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the purpose of pre-registration?',array['to make it harder to present exploration as prediction','to increase sample sizes automatically','to speed up publication','to keep methods secret']::text[],'A','It makes it ''harder to present an exploratory fishing expedition as a confirmed prediction.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','The ''replication crisis'' refers to the discovery that a surprisingly large proportion of published findings, particularly in psychology and medicine, could not be reproduced when other researchers repeated the experiments. Several factors have been blamed. Journals have historically preferred striking, positive results, giving researchers an incentive to analyse their data in many ways until something appears significant. Small sample sizes make chance findings more likely. In response, many journals now encourage ''pre-registration'', in which scientists publicly state their hypotheses and methods before collecting data, making it harder to present an exploratory fishing expedition as a confirmed prediction.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:8083fbf4eb7b','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why do small sample sizes matter?',array['They make results easy to reproduce.','They are banned by journals.','They make chance findings more likely.','They make experiments too expensive.']::text[],'C','''Small sample sizes make chance findings more likely.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','The ''replication crisis'' refers to the discovery that a surprisingly large proportion of published findings, particularly in psychology and medicine, could not be reproduced when other researchers repeated the experiments. Several factors have been blamed. Journals have historically preferred striking, positive results, giving researchers an incentive to analyse their data in many ways until something appears significant. Small sample sizes make chance findings more likely. In response, many journals now encourage ''pre-registration'', in which scientists publicly state their hypotheses and methods before collecting data, making it harder to present an exploratory fishing expedition as a confirmed prediction.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:4f004dbd005e','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did four-day week trials report?',array['Salaries were reduced by 20%.','Output was largely maintained and stress fell.','Productivity fell sharply.','Staff took more sick days.']::text[],'B','''Output was largely maintained, while staff reported lower stress and fewer sick days.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Proponents of the four-day working week argue that productivity, rather than hours, should be the measure of work. Trials in which employees kept their full salary while working 80 per cent of their previous hours have reported that output was largely maintained, while staff reported lower stress and fewer sick days. Sceptics caution against generalising from these results. The companies that volunteered were, they note, disproportionately office-based firms already inclined towards flexible working; it is far less obvious how the model would apply to hospitals, schools or shops, where a reduction in hours means either hiring additional staff or reducing the service provided.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:be20d451d6e0','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why do sceptics doubt the trials can be generalised?',array['The trials were too long.','Employees were paid less.','Hospitals reported better results.','The firms that took part were not typical.']::text[],'D','''The companies that volunteered were... disproportionately office-based firms already inclined towards flexible working.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Proponents of the four-day working week argue that productivity, rather than hours, should be the measure of work. Trials in which employees kept their full salary while working 80 per cent of their previous hours have reported that output was largely maintained, while staff reported lower stress and fewer sick days. Sceptics caution against generalising from these results. The companies that volunteered were, they note, disproportionately office-based firms already inclined towards flexible working; it is far less obvious how the model would apply to hospitals, schools or shops, where a reduction in hours means either hiring additional staff or reducing the service provided.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:bd4945d07501','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to sceptics, what would hospitals face with fewer hours?',array['higher productivity','hiring more staff or reducing service','no change at all','lower salaries for doctors']::text[],'B','''A reduction in hours means either hiring additional staff or reducing the service provided.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Proponents of the four-day working week argue that productivity, rather than hours, should be the measure of work. Trials in which employees kept their full salary while working 80 per cent of their previous hours have reported that output was largely maintained, while staff reported lower stress and fewer sick days. Sceptics caution against generalising from these results. The companies that volunteered were, they note, disproportionately office-based firms already inclined towards flexible working; it is far less obvious how the model would apply to hospitals, schools or shops, where a reduction in hours means either hiring additional staff or reducing the service provided.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:7a1768344abd','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What does ''proponents'' mean in the text?',array['supporters','critics','researchers','employers']::text[],'A','Proponents are people who argue in favour of an idea.',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Proponents of the four-day working week argue that productivity, rather than hours, should be the measure of work. Trials in which employees kept their full salary while working 80 per cent of their previous hours have reported that output was largely maintained, while staff reported lower stress and fewer sick days. Sceptics caution against generalising from these results. The companies that volunteered were, they note, disproportionately office-based firms already inclined towards flexible working; it is far less obvious how the model would apply to hospitals, schools or shops, where a reduction in hours means either hiring additional staff or reducing the service provided.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:1502b64c1531','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did the letters reveal about the novelist?',array['She worked in the archive.','She destroyed her own novels.','She was connected to leading reformers and wrote political pamphlets.','She was a famous best-selling author.']::text[],'C','The letters showed regular contact with reformers and anonymous pamphlets.',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','When the family of a minor Victorian novelist donated a trunk of letters to a provincial archive, few expected it to attract much attention. Yet the correspondence revealed that she had been in regular contact with several of the leading reformers of her day and had anonymously written pamphlets that were widely circulated at the time. Scholars now argue that her marginal status in literary histories owes less to the quality of her work than to the conventions of the period, which discouraged women from publishing political writing under their own names. The discovery has prompted a broader reassessment of which voices were preserved, and why.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:930e62e31a3e','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why, according to scholars, was she marginal in literary history?',array['She wrote only in letters.','Her writing was of poor quality.','Her family hid her letters.','Conventions discouraged women from publishing political work under their names.']::text[],'D','Her status ''owes less to the quality of her work than to the conventions of the period''.',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','When the family of a minor Victorian novelist donated a trunk of letters to a provincial archive, few expected it to attract much attention. Yet the correspondence revealed that she had been in regular contact with several of the leading reformers of her day and had anonymously written pamphlets that were widely circulated at the time. Scholars now argue that her marginal status in literary histories owes less to the quality of her work than to the conventions of the period, which discouraged women from publishing political writing under their own names. The discovery has prompted a broader reassessment of which voices were preserved, and why.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:47da85572c62','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What broader effect has the discovery had?',array['a ban on anonymous writing','the closure of the archive','a new novel about her life','a reassessment of whose voices were preserved']::text[],'D','''The discovery has prompted a broader reassessment of which voices were preserved, and why.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','When the family of a minor Victorian novelist donated a trunk of letters to a provincial archive, few expected it to attract much attention. Yet the correspondence revealed that she had been in regular contact with several of the leading reformers of her day and had anonymously written pamphlets that were widely circulated at the time. Scholars now argue that her marginal status in literary histories owes less to the quality of her work than to the conventions of the period, which discouraged women from publishing political writing under their own names. The discovery has prompted a broader reassessment of which voices were preserved, and why.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:bb5da1a610db','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were expectations when the trunk was donated?',array['Everyone expected a famous discovery.','Few expected much interest.','The archive refused it.','Scholars had been searching for it.']::text[],'B','''Few expected it to attract much attention.''',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','When the family of a minor Victorian novelist donated a trunk of letters to a provincial archive, few expected it to attract much attention. Yet the correspondence revealed that she had been in regular contact with several of the leading reformers of her day and had anonymously written pamphlets that were widely circulated at the time. Scholars now argue that her marginal status in literary histories owes less to the quality of her work than to the conventions of the period, which discouraged women from publishing political writing under their own names. The discovery has prompted a broader reassessment of which voices were preserved, and why.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:e0aca1d394f7','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which phrase best describes the tone of the nudge text?',array['humorous','entirely negative','balanced, presenting arguments for and against','strongly in favour']::text[],'C','The text gives advocates'' and critics'' views.',array['exam','reading']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06','Governments have increasingly turned to ''nudges'' — small changes in how choices are presented — to influence behaviour without banning anything or raising prices. Making pension saving the default option, for instance, so that employees must actively opt out rather than opt in, has dramatically increased participation in the UK. Advocates describe this as ''libertarian paternalism'': people remain free to choose, yet are steered towards decisions that serve their long-term interests. Critics, however, question who decides what those interests are, and warn that techniques which work precisely because people do not notice them sit uneasily with democratic transparency. Others argue that nudges are too modest to tackle problems such as obesity or climate change, and may serve as a convenient excuse for avoiding more effective but less popular regulation.',null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:c6281fe3988b','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','___ to the public in 1759, the British Museum was the first national public museum.',array['Having opening','Opened','Opening','To open']::text[],'B','A past participle clause with passive meaning: it was opened.',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:cdbb6f64cc4e','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','All things ___, the event was a success.',array['considering','consider','considered','to consider']::text[],'C','''All things considered'' is a fixed participle phrase.',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:d723e87f26dd','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','The longer the negotiations drag on, the ___ likely a deal becomes.',array['fewer','less','least','lesser']::text[],'B','''The + comparative..., the less likely...''.',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:b5c2ec9a1671','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','Such ___ the demand that tickets sold out in minutes.',array['were','had','did','was']::text[],'D','''Such was the demand that...'' (inversion with ''such'').',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:d700c07b2394','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','She left the company, ___ surprised everyone.',array['what','which','who','that']::text[],'B','''Which'' can refer to a whole previous clause.',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:0f931b367ab1','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','Were you ___ the position, when could you start?',array['to be offered','being offered','to offer','offered']::text[],'A','''Were + subject + to be + past participle'' for a hypothetical passive.',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:b5b02ef625f2','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','He must have been exhausted, ___ worked for 20 hours straight.',array['to have','being','having','had']::text[],'C','A perfect participle clause gives the reason.',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:35f2b6eb22a4','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','I''ll lend you my car on condition ___ you return it by six.',array['if','of','which','that']::text[],'D','''On condition that'' + clause.',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
  ('ai:ai-batch-06:4e8557245733','C1'::public.cefr_level,'grammar','Only after the contract had been signed ___ the hidden costs.',array['discovered they','did they discover','they did discover','they discovered']::text[],'B','''Only after...'' at the start causes inversion.',array['exam','grammar']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-06',null,null,null,null,'exam'),
